---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const M = "server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py";
const W = "server/src/iki_server/runs/workflows.py";
const T = "server/src/iki_server/runs/triggers.py";

const H = {
  short: "Runs that other things launch",
  how: "Workflows and triggers",
  example: "A condition, and a schedule",
  rules: "Steps, approvals, loops and schedules",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A workflow is a DAG of ordinary runs: each step is launched by the same <code>launch</code> as
      any run, as the person who started the workflow, in its project, once the steps it takes from
      have succeeded. A trigger launches an action when records change, or on a schedule, once a
      project's Admin turns it on there. Neither has an execution path of its own, so every launch
      check, pin, quota and approval applies to their runs too (<Adr n={[11, 79, 80, 81]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <h3>Workflows</h3>
    <p>
      Starting a workflow checks its inputs and params as a launch would, and what its steps
      include of its own inputs, then keeps a <code>WorkflowRun</code> with the workflow as
      installed, so its steps run as it said then, and its inputs at their revisions. One consent
      covers what its steps send to outside APIs (<Path p={W} sym="def start(" />, <Path
        p={M}
        sym="class WorkflowRun("
      />). <code>advance</code> moves it on when one of its runs ends, when it starts or is
      cancelled, and from the task runners' housekeeping. It locks the workflow run, so two never
      launch a step twice. Each waiting step whose earlier steps succeeded is checked against its{" "}
      <code>when</code> conditions, may wait for approval, and is then launched, one run or one per
      entity (<Path p={W} sym="def _advance(" />, <Path p={W} sym="def _launch(" />).
    </p>

    <h3>Triggers</h3>
    <p>
      A plugin declares triggers on <code>entity.created</code>, <code>entity.updated</code> (for
      given fields), <code>entity.state_changed</code>, <code>entity.registered</code> or a{" "}
      <code>schedule</code>. None runs until a project's Admin turns it on in that project. A change
      that fires one is recorded as a <code>TriggerFiring</code> in the change's own transaction, so a
      firing exists exactly when its change does; a task launches its run once the change commits,
      and task runners sweep each minute for any left behind (<Path p={T} sym="def noticed(" />, <Path
        p={T}
        sym="def _fire("
      />). Its runs are launched as the Admin who turned it on, with <code>via="trigger"</code>, so
      those on the container or vm tier, or calling outside APIs, wait for that Admin's approval
      (<PageLink to="Launching a run" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      The spec's test plugin <Path p="spec/tests/plugins/protein/workflows/align_then_weigh.yaml" label="align_then_weigh" />{" "}
      computes a protein's properties only if its alignment with another is close enough. The
      second step reads a metric the first step's run reported:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="spec/tests/plugins/protein/workflows/align_then_weigh.yaml" start="^steps:" rest note="from steps: on" />
    <p>
      If the alignment's <code>score</code> is below the param, <code>weigh</code> is skipped with{" "}
      <code>condition_false</code>; if the run didn't report it, with <code>condition_unknown</code>,
      never taken as false silently (<Path p={W} sym="def _unmet(" />). A scheduled trigger takes no
      input; this one imports what a LIMS changed since its last successful run:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="examples/plugins/lims_connector/triggers/import_nightly.yaml" start="^on:" rest note="from on: on" />
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Step states" open>
        <p>
          A step is waiting, awaiting approval, running, succeeded, failed, cancelled, skipped (an
          earlier step didn't succeed, its condition didn't hold, or the workflow was cancelled) or
          declined. A workflow re-run carries over the steps that succeeded and runs the rest again
          (<Path p={M} sym="class StepState(" />, <Path p={W} sym="def rerun(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A step can wait for approval">
        <p>
          A step with an <code>approval</code> waits, when its turn comes, for someone with that role
          in the project other than whoever started the workflow, who are told. Nobody deciding within
          30 days declines it, and the workflow fails (<Path p={W} sym="def _ask(" />, <Path
            p={M}
            sym="APPROVAL_EXPIRES_AFTER = timedelta(days=30)"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Triggered runs fire nothing">
        <p>
          What a triggered run writes never fires a trigger, so a trigger can't fire itself, or
          another that fires it back; chaining belongs in workflows (<Path p={T} sym="def writing(" />).
          A trigger may give other inputs the records related to the one that fired it, through the
          graph (<Adr n="0123" />, <Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/triggers.py" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Calculations follow what they read">
        <p>
          A trigger's <code>watch_included</code> names fields of records its input includes; when
          one changes, the calculation is queued again for the records that reference it, in the
          projects that turned it on (<Path p={T} sym="def source_changed(" />). A{" "}
          <code>recheck</code> trigger runs a deterministic backend calculation, which updates only
          computed fields of the edited record, under the editor's own credential as they save
          (<Path p={T} sym="def recheck_edit(" />). Their results are written only if the records
          they read haven't changed since (<PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Schedules">
        <p>
          A schedule is cron's five fields in an IANA time zone, at most every five minutes. Its runs
          are launched as a service account the Admin picked, on that Admin's authority: both must
          still be able to run the tier, or Iki turns the trigger off and tells the project's Admins.
          Each time runs at most once and never on top of the last; times missed while Iki was down
          run once. Each run's input names the time it was for and the last successful run's
          (<code>since</code>), and scheduled runs never reuse earlier results (<Adr n="0081" />,{" "}
          <Path p={T} sym="def tick(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <h3>Routes</h3>
    <Routes tags={["workflows"]} />
    <Routes tags={["triggers"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={W} /></li>
          <li><Path p={T} /></li>
          <li><Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/workflows.py" /></li>
          <li><Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/triggers.py" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0011" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0079" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0080" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0081" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
